    {
      "title": "Nasa images show 60-foot-wide crater on moon after SpaceX rocket crash",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/nasa-images-show-60-foot-wide-crater-on-moon-after-spacex-rocket-crash-126081900079_1.html",
      "description": "A NASA spacecraft circling the moon is providing the sharpest views yet of the crater carved by a crashing SpaceX rocket.\nThe space agency released before-and-after photos of the impact area on Tuesday.\nThe Falcon rocket's upper stage plowed into the moon at 5,400 mph (8,700 kph) on Aug. 5 after drifting through space for more than a year. It launched a pair of private moon landers with experiments and even a tiny rover in 2025 as part of NASA's push to commercialize lunar exploration.\nBased on these new photos by NASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter, the fresh crater appears to be 60 feet (18 meters) across and less than 10 feet (3 meters) deep, according to scientists.\nDark and bright streaks are clearly visible emanating like butterfly wings from the crater. The darker lines represent excavated material that was close to the surface and shaped by eons of solar wind, cosmic rays and micrometeorite strikes. The brighter rays indicate fresh rocks and dirt that were hurled from farth",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
